                <pb facs="0219" n="117" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg117"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">715.1715/89: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Jones</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-01-14T18:00:00-05:48"><hi rendition="#italic">January 14,
                                1919, 6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received
                            January 16, 3.59 p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>In further reference to your telegrams of December 14 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >21?</hi>], 4 p.m. and January 8, 7 p.m., I have received from the Minister
                    for Foreign Affairs note as final reply of Bertrand, substance of which is as
                    follows: “The [construction] of telegraph lines to Las Trojas was commenced long
                    before the present difficulty [over the] boundary with the Nicaraguan Government
                    arose. Lack of materials caused suspension of line, construction [of] which has
                    not been carried to Las Trojas but to Chichicaste far from disputed territory.
                    Bertrand again protests firmest determination to maintain <hi
                        rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> and accede to friendly suggestions of
                    the United States.</p>
                <p>The Honduranean Government makes a complaint that Nicaragua violates [agreed] <hi
                        rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> by granting lumber concessions in the
                    disputed territory, claiming to have conclusive proof. [Requests] the Government
                    of the United States make representations to the Nicaraguan Government that it
                    should desist from violation of <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> [garbled
                    group]. The Minister for Foreign Affairs showed me documents supporting above.
                    Have heard nothing since.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jones</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
